how did a video game ruin the ending of  serenity if you've come for some of the  worst twists in movie history stick  around because you're in the right place  batman begins got people to take the  caped crusader seriously at the movies  again and the dark knight was an  award-winning sensation that's why hype  was understandably huge for the dark  knight rises the final film in  christopher nolan's epic batman trilogy  unfortunately the third film was in many  ways a step down from its predecessors  there are several distracting plot holes  such as bruce wayne's inexplicable  escape to gotham after being imprisoned  on the other side of the planet though  his excessive wealth could be enough  explanation there's a lot to love about  the dark knight rises it's a bombastic  thriller filled with excellent visuals  and great performances especially from  tom hardy's bane the twists weren't  great though the reveal that joseph  gordon-levitt's character's name is  robin made some people roll their eyes  but it ultimately didn't affect the plot  however when miranda tate stabs batman  and reveals herself to be raza ghoul's  daughter talia who has bane on a leash  and is carrying on the legacy of the  league of shadows it was a little harder  to ignore upon the film's release some  fans complained that the twist was too  predictable while others argued that it  undermined bane's abilities as a super  villain regardless of reasoning it's  generally agreed that the ending of the  dark knight rises didn't exactly live up  to the expectations set by its  predecessors  m night shyamalan is a name synonymous  with twist endings shyamalan has had one  of the most bizarre and noteworthy  careers in hollywood history the sixth  sense unbreakable and signs were all  wildly popular establishing him as one  of the hottest directors around at the  start of the millennium a decade later  however most of the moviegoing public's  goodwill towards him had evaporated  thanks to critical duds like the  happening the last airbender and after  earth what happened between the  unforgettable reveal of the sixth sense  and the cringe-inducing cheese of the  last airbender is anyone's guess we do  know where the first signs of trouble  started popping up though 2004's the  village released two years after signs  the village follows a group of 19th  century pennsylvanian townsfolk who are  stalked by strange monsters that lurk in  the woods audiences couldn't wait to see  what neck snapping twist shyamalan had  in store but when it was revealed that  the film actually takes place in the  present day and that the monsters were  just the town elders in costumes most  people weren't impressed still shyamalan  refuses to let bad reviews stop him from  writing crazy twists his most recent  project 2021's old features a similarly  baffling reveal of its own jk rowling  isn't the only one making things worse  for the legacy of the harry potter  franchise the other folks behind the  fantastic beasts movie are helping out  too the first movie in the spin-off  series in which british wizard newt  scamander tries to recapture his escaped  magical creatures in new york city  definitely has its moments redmayne  charms in the lead role his sidekicks  are a blast and the various fantastic  beasts certainly live up to the film's  title however the movie was never really  about capturing billy wigs and  bowtruckles scamander and his friends  have to get to the bottom of an  anti-muggle plot orchestrated by  percival graves the director of magical  security when graves is apprehended his  true identity is revealed he's actually  the legendary dark wizard grindelwald in  disguise that kind of twist might have  worked in a lesser franchise but the  reveal cheapened grindelwald as a  character moving forward worse still it  set up a decidedly unmagical letdown of  a sequel in fantastic beasts the crimes  of brindlewald  it's no surprise that a film about  criminal magicians who rob the rich and  give to the poor is going to have a lot  of twists with a description like that  the only twist we wouldn't see coming is  a straightforward narrative but of  course now you see me has plenty of  twists in store the four horsemen a  group of expert illusionists rob wealthy  benefactors and shower their audiences  with money fbi agent dylan rhodes leads  the cryptic investigation into their  crimes believing them to be members of a  secret society of magicians known as the  eye it's certainly a unique idea and the  a-list cast is an excellent selling  point unfortunately the movie eventually  starts to sag under the weight of all  the fake outs improbable escapes and  double crosses but none of them are  nearly as ludicrous as the final reveal  welcome to the eye  yes rhodes the fbi agent hunting down  the four horsemen is not only a magician  himself but the son of another legendary  illusionist and the mysterious  mastermind behind the crew's latest epic  heist farfetch doesn't begin to describe  this laughable development  like many other movies on this list  orphan has a promising start after their  child is stillborn kate and john coleman  decide to adopt a precocious  nine-year-old girl named esther  the thing is esther is far from a normal  kid  some problematic behavior might be  expected of any child who's waiting for  a home in the foster care system but  esther is a different kind of troubled  she interrupts the colemans during  intimate moments and reveals an alarming  level of knowledge about sexual topics  she scares the coleman's other children  by killing animals in front of them  sister abigail who runs the orphanage  esther was adopted from later informs  kate and john that tragedy always seems  to follow esther around  thanks sister that would have been nice  to know from the beginning at least she  let them know before esther was able to  kill her kate is quickly convinced that  esther is sinister but it takes jon  longer to accept the malevolence behind  their new daughter's behavior jon  finally admits that something is off  when esther tries to seduce him but it's  pretty much too late at that point  that's when we learn that esther isn't  nine at all and that she isn't esther  either she's a 33 year old estonian  woman with a hormone disorder the  bizarre reveal certainly explains a lot  but that doesn't make it any less  ridiculous christmas movies don't  usually wow critics and are often little  more than heartwarming distractions for  everyone else but a christmas movie that  takes its premise from song lyrics now  that's a recipe for narrative disaster  enter last christmas this story follows  kate an aspiring singer who lives with  her parents after losing her job and  getting thrown out of her lover's house  by his partner the one bright spot in  her life is tom a mysterious man with  whom she develops an intimate romance  after the two connect she reveals she  had a heart transplant a year earlier  one day kate visits tom's apartment and  discovers it's being sold she then  learns that the last owner died in a  bicycle accident the previous christmas  kate then puts two and two together and  realizes tom is the previous owner and  when he died she received his heart so  he was a ghost all along great after  learning this kate tries to focus on  doing good for others as tom would have  wanted she spends more time outside and  sings a certain wham song at a homeless  shelter while reminiscing about the good  times she had with her boyfriend whom  she never actually met what were the  lyrics again last christmas i gave you  my heart get it yeah we do too  john cuzak leads an excellent ensemble  cast and identity a slasher who done it  very loosely based on agatha christie's  and then there were none the film  follows 10 strangers stranded in a hotel  who are mysteriously killed off one by  one as the story progresses viewers are  shown flashbacks depicting important  events that supposedly occurred shortly  before the guests arrived the big twist  none of this actually happened malcolm  rivers is a condemned serial killer due  to die in less than two days his lawyers  try to stay the execution leading to an  11th hour trial in which it's revealed  that rivers has dissociative identity  disorder the hotel guest killings  represent rivers killing his different  personalities twists that are integral  to the plot like the one in identity  always work better than twists slapped  on at the last minute to shock audiences  and elevate a lousy boring story this  one is certainly thought-provoking but  ultimately too absurd and far-fetched to  stick the landing  remember me as a sweet but ultimately  forgettable rom-com for most of its run  time tyler hawkins works at the nyu  bookstore  and audits classes there while ali is a  student one night tyler and his roommate  aiden are arrested by an nypd detective  named neil after getting involved in a  fight that someone else started when  aiden discovers neil as ally's father he  convinces tyler to seduce and then dump  her to get revenge instead tyler and  ally predictably fall in love for real  after bonding over their shared trauma  and daddy issues it turns out tyler's  brother had taken his own life years  earlier leading his father to focus on  raising his sister who struggles  socially and needs support meanwhile ali  reveals her mother was mugged and  murdered when she was younger by this  point some viewers might be rolling  their eyes at all the cheap emotional  manipulation which uses extreme tragedy  as a stand-in for more subtle character  development but that's nothing compared  to what's coming next after all of these  plot threads have been resolved tyler  looks out the window of his new office  located in the north tower of the world  trade center on the morning of september  11 2001 it doesn't get more clumsily  manipulative than that  not unlike the star wars prequel trilogy  the sequels had a rocky time and divided  fans the force awakens was a welcome  return to form for many but too similar  to a new hope for others the last jedi  delighted critics with loads of fresh  thought-provoking ideas but those same  risky storytelling choices left some  fans with a bad taste in their mouths  and then there's the rise of skywalker  which pleased almost no one one of  episode nine's many narrative missteps  was how it handled the long-running  question of rey's parentage the last  jedi challenged the trope that fantasy  protagonists especially in star wars  must have a noble lineage after kylo ren  tells our heroine that she's the  descendant of nobodies however the rise  of skywalker decided to retcon that  development revealing rey to be a  relative of one of the most fearsome  figures in the galaxy far far away  emperor palpatine  you're his granddaughter  you  are a palpatine  the filmmakers behind the rise of  skywalker were almost certainly trying  to recreate the iconic reveal from the  empire strikes back but they were unable  to replicate its success instead the  sequel trilogy serves as a cautionary  tale of what happens when two filmmakers  with opposing visions are forced to  share a multi-billion dollar franchise  with the release of both iron man and  the dark knight 2008 was a great year  for superhero movies unfortunately the  ending of peter berg's hancock meant  that they couldn't all be winners will  smith plays the titular hancock an  abrasive alcoholic superhuman who can't  remember his own secret identity jason  bateman plays ray embry a pr specialist  who volunteers to help hancock  revitalize his image after the hero  saves his life with that unique and  seemingly fun premise the pieces were  all there for a recommendable one-off  action-comedy superhero flag but then  the second half of the movie happens and  we find out rey's wife is also an  immortal superhuman and she's also  hancock's ex-wife it turns out that  superheroes only have powers when  they're apart and his proximity to her  made him vulnerable to an attack 80  years ago resulting in his  hospitalization and amnesia viewers  generally agreed that hancock's two  halves felt disjointed which isn't a  surprise considering that the film was  stuck in development hell for 12 years  matthew mcconaughey and anne hathaway  had perfect chemistry in interstellar so  it was a bit of a surprise that 2019's  serenity missed the mark as much as it  did the plot sounds intriguing enough at  least on paper boat captain dill baker  is asked by his ex-wife karen to save  her and her son patrick by murdering her  abusive husband frank she books frank  for a ride on dill's boat for a week  where it should be easy to toss him  overboard with no witnesses but dill is  forced to wrestle with his conscience  over whether or not he should go through  with it after all so what's the big  twist does karen have a hidden agenda  has frank been dead the whole time is  dill secretly grindelwald in disguise no  no and no it turns out that dill is a  playable video game character designed  by patrick only after dill completes his  in-game objective does patrick work up  the courage to kill frank in real life  in his review of the film for the new  york times critic wesley morris said of  the big reveal it's like the worst of  christopher nolan m night shyamalan and  the condolence cards section at  walgreens  check out one of her newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

